WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.300
We'd like to think audible for supporting PBS Digital Studios

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:16.940
Do you remember the very first job you ever had?

00:00:16.949 --> 00:00:20.910
Maybe it was bussing tables, bagging groceries,
or working as a lifeguard.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:25.150
You put in your hours, keeping track of how
much you expected to make, and when that first

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:29.630
paycheck arrived, you opened it up and - surprise!

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:32.420
Some of that money had already been spent
for you.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:36.780
If you’ve ever looked closely at your paystub,
you probably noticed that Uncle Sam’s portion

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:43.500
is broken into two main categories: The Federal
Insurance Contribution ACT (aka FICA) and

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.920
Federal Income tax.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:50.309
The first one is essentially a federal insurance
program for the elderly, and helps pay for

00:00:50.309 --> 00:00:54.120
retirement and medical programs for older
Americans.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.940
Income tax rates have varied wildly throughout
history.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:01.980
Before 1913, they were actually forbidden
by the U.S. Constitution.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:03.540
Thanks, 16th Amendment!

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:12.120
As recently as 1981, the top tax rate was
70% for singles earning over $108,300.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:18.940
And the highest rate on record was in 1945,
with a top rate of 94% for the ultra-wealthy!

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:21.720
These days, tax rates aren’t quite so high.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.980
But no matter how much the Federal Government
takes from your paycheck, you should know

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.680
what it’s being spent on.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.980
Have you ever stopped to figure out exactly
WHAT is being bought with all that money you

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:34.060
send to Washington each year?

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.240
I think it’s time to…

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.630
RUN THE NUMBERS!

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:40.970
This is Tabitha.

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:44.659
She works in H.R. and made $40,000 in 2017.

00:01:44.659 --> 00:01:51.820
After exemptions and deductions, her taxable
income is $29,600, placing her in the 15%

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:53.520
marginal tax bracket.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.700
Tabitha lives in a state with no state income
tax, so at the end of the year, she ended

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:01.619
up paying $7,038​ in Federal Taxes.

00:02:01.619 --> 00:02:05.330
Nearly half goes toward Social Security and
Medicare.

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:10.369
These taxes, known as payroll taxes, are spent
on health insurance and income for the disabled

00:02:10.369 --> 00:02:13.780
and people in retirement, like Tabitha’s
Grandparents.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:18.560
Gram-Gram and Paw-paw paid their FICA taxes
when they were Tabitha’s age, and now they’re

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:21.780
enjoying the benefits, just like Tabitha will
someday.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:27.950
That leaves $3,978 of Tabitha’s Federal
Income Tax left to be spent:

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:32.790
The largest chunk of your Federal Income Tax
goes to medical programs that help low-income

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:35.580
families, like Medicaid and CHIP.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:41.660
That means that, including FICA, almost 60% of Tabitha’s federal taxes are basically going towards

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:43.280
insurance programs.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:48.250
$930 of Tabitha’s salary supports men and
women enlisted in the armed forces, plus it

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:51.600
pays for fighter jets, submarines, and Pentagon
staff.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.970
Tabitha may not realize it, but she’s on
the hook for America’s loans.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:03:02.520
$525 of her income taxes goes to pay back
the debt the government borrows to say afloat.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:03.680
Plus interest.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:07.580
$298 goes to Unemployment and Labor programs…

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:12.720
$238 to Veteran’s Benefits, like VA Hospitals
and veteran job training programs.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.530
$179 goes to Food &amp; Agriculture…

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:20.799
And $167 of Tabitha’s salary helps keep
the government running, funding agencies like

00:03:20.799 --> 00:03:26.090
the FBI, Border Patrol, Federal Prisons,
and even the salaries of her Senators.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:35.010
Tabitha spends $127 on Transportation agencies
like the FAA and TSA, and $111 on Education,

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:38.090
including Pell Grants and the National Endowment
for the Arts.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:44.600
She gives $83 a year to Housing &amp; Community
programs, $63 a year to Energy and the

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:45.760
Environment…

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:51.180
$51 a year for International Affairs like the
State Department and the Peace Corps...and

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:57.320
$39 a year to fund America’s science
programs, which includes all federal scientific

00:03:57.329 --> 00:03:59.730
research and space exploration.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:04.000
Although it might seem like those dollars
just vanish from your paycheck, the money

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:09.700
you pay to the Federal Government affects
real people, and changes our world every day.

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:13.200
It would be hard to find even one of these
expenses that doesn’t touch your life or

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.900
the life of someone you love.

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:15.900
And guess what?

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:18.280
These numbers aren’t set in stone.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:23.550
You can contact your local representative,
national lobby groups, and educate yourself.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:28.460
The money our government collects in taxes
doesn’t belong to any one person or agency,

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:34.280
it belongs to all of us, ​collectively
​and we should all get a say in how it’s spent.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:36.040
And that’s our two cents.

